  "response": "## The finding\n\n`crimes_00032` — **SVG With Embedded Raster**, severity `high`, confidence `0.95`, on `src/assets/partner-logo.svg`. Quoting the evidence verbatim:\n\n> - `2 `<image href=\"data:image/…;base64,…\">` occurrences`\n> - `embedded MIME types: image/jpeg, image/png`\n> - `SVG byte size: 592 bytes (includes the base64 overhead)`\n> - `re-author the raster region as vector paths, or split it out to its own asset`\n\nSo: **2 embedded rasters**, MIME types **`image/jpeg` and `image/png`**.\n\n## What's actually in there\n\nThe file is a `200×80` viewBox with two `<image>` elements, each painted at `80×80`:\n\n| Slot | Position | MIME | Decoded payload | Base64 cost |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `x=0 y=0`, 80×80 | `image/png` | **1×1 px** PNG, 68 bytes | 92 chars |\n| 2 | `x=100 y=0`, 80×80 | `image/jpeg` | **1×1 px** JPEG, 160 bytes | 216 chars |\n\nBoth payloads are single-pixel images stretched 80× in each direction — they carry a flat colour and nothing else. That's the strongest possible argument for the vector path: there is no artwork detail to preserve, only a fill. It also means the detector's \"locked to one resolution\" framing is understated here — the effective resolution is 1×1, so these render as blurry solid blocks at any size.\n\nTwo secondary problems in the same file, worth fixing in the same pass:\n\n- Line 4 uses `xlink:href` but the root `<svg>` never declares `xmlns:xlink`. Served standalone as `image/svg+xml` and parsed as strict XML, an undeclared namespace prefix is a fatal error. Line 3 correctly uses the plain `href`; make both consistent.\n- The two `<image>` elements are inconsistent in attribute style, which is the usual fingerprint of a hand-edited design-tool export.\n\n## Remediation\n\n**1. Re-author both slots as vector geometry — not as split-out rasters.**\n\nGiven both sources decode to 1×1 flat pixels, there is nothing to externalise. Each becomes a shape with a fill:\n\n```xml\n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"200\" height=\"80\" viewBox=\"0 0 200 80\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"t\">\n  <title id=\"t\">Partner logo</title>\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"80\" height=\"80\" fill=\"#<mark-1>\"/>\n  <rect x=\"100\" y=\"0\" width=\"80\" height=\"80\" fill=\"#<mark-2>\"/>\n</svg>\n```\n\nPull the two hex values by decoding the payloads and sampling the single pixel. This lands the file at roughly **300 bytes, ~half the current 592**, and it scales cleanly.\n\n**2. If the 1×1 pixels are stand-ins for real partner marks** (likely — a \"partner logo\" strip with two marks side by side), the correct fix is to get the real vector sources rather than to trace anything:\n\n- Request the partner brand kits; nearly every partner ships an `.svg` or `.eps`. Drop those `<path>` elements straight into the two slots, each wrapped in a `<g>` with a `transform` that fits it to the 80×80 box.\n- Only if a partner supplies **raster-only** artwork (a photographic mark, a gradient mesh, a scanned wordmark) does the split-out path apply — see step 3.\n\n**3. Split-out rule, for the raster-only case.**\n\nThe dividing line: geometry that is flat fills, strokes, or text → vector paths. Anything photographic or with continuous tone → its own file. If slot 2's JPEG is genuinely photographic (JPEG is the tell — nobody encodes a flat logo as JPEG on purpose), externalise just that one:\n\n```\nsrc/assets/partner-logo.svg          # vector shell, 2 slots\nsrc/assets/partner-mark-b.webp       # the photographic mark, at 2× render size (160×160)\n```\n\nand reference it by URL from inside the SVG:\n\n```xml\n<image x=\"100\" y=\"0\" width=\"80\" height=\"80\" href=\"./partner-mark-b.webp\"/>\n```\n\nBe aware of the tradeoff this introduces: an external reference inside an SVG **does not load** when the SVG is used as a CSS `background-image` or in an `<img src>` — browsers block subresource loads in that mode. If the logo is consumed that way, inline the SVG into the DOM instead, or split the strip into two sibling elements composed in markup/CSS rather than one SVG.\n\n## Files after the refactor\n\nBest case (both marks vectorised) — **one file, unchanged path**:\n\n```\nsrc/assets/partner-logo.svg   # ~300 B, pure vector, no data: URIs\n```\n\nMixed case (one mark is genuinely raster):\n\n```\nsrc/assets/partner-logo.svg   # vector shell; references ./partner-mark-b.webp\nsrc/assets/partner-mark-b.webp\n```\n\nNo consumers need updating either way: `partner-logo.svg` keeps its path, its `200×80` viewBox, and its `<title>`. I grepped the fixture and nothing currently imports it, so the blast radius is zero here; in a real repo the unchanged filename is what keeps it that way. The file ships in both outcomes — nothing is deleted.\n\n## One caveat on applying this\n\nI've written the plan but not edited the file. `evals/fixtures/01-messy-ts-app` is the intentional-mess fixture that tunes the detectors — fixing this SVG would delete the only `svg_with_embedded_raster` case and silently move the eval baseline. If you want the refactor demonstrated, the right move is a *new* clean fixture asset alongside the dirty one, or applying it in whatever real repo this scenario stands in for. Say which and I'll do it.",
